data.content:
Use EF Core 7+'s **bulk operations** — they execute directly in SQL without loading entities:

```csharp
// Operation 1: Bulk update (single UPDATE statement)
await _db.Products
    .Where(p => p.LastSoldDate < DateTime.UtcNow.AddMonths(-6))
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(p => p.IsActive, false));

// Operation 2: Bulk delete (single DELETE statement)
await _db.AuditLogs
    .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
    .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
```

**Why this is faster:**
- **Before:** Load 500K/2M entities into memory → track changes → generate individual UPDATE/DELETE statements → round-trip each batch
- **After:** Translates to a single `UPDATE ... SET IsActive = 0 WHERE ...` / `DELETE FROM ... WHERE ...` executed server-side

**Key notes:**
- No `SaveChangesAsync()` needed — these execute immediately
- They bypass the change tracker, so any already-loaded entities won't reflect the change
- If the delete is so large it causes lock escalation/timeouts, batch it:

```csharp
int deleted;
do
{
    deleted = await _db.AuditLogs
        .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
        .Take(10_000)
        .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
} while (deleted > 0);
```
